.TH APPFS 2
.SH NAME
appfs \- serve a program's own files, posted
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "appfs.m";
appfs := load Appfs Appfs->PATH;

Req: adt {
	file:	string;
	data:	string;
	reply:	chan of string;
};

init:	fn(): string;
serve:	fn(spec: string, files: array of string, reqs: chan of ref Req):
		(string, ref Sys->FD, string);
event:	fn(msg: string);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
A program with something to show another, an agent's tool or Lucifer,
names its
.IR files ;
.B serve
serves them, with
.B event
beside them, as a 9P tree posted as
.BI #s spec /fs
.RB ( fs.1
for a second instance; see
.IR post9p (2)).
It returns the path posted and a tether, or
.B nil
and the reason.
.PP
A read or write of one of the files reaches the program as a
.B Req
on
.IR reqs ,
to be answered in its own loop from what it holds at that moment.
For a read
.B data
is nil and the program replies with the text; a read is made once an
open, at its offset 0, so a reader taking it in pieces sees one text.
For a write
.B data
is what was written, and the program replies nil when it is done or a
reason, which is the write's error.
Nothing is written ahead of time and nothing is polled.
.PP
.B Event
gives every reader of
.B event
the line
.IR msg :
a read of
.B event
waits for the next, each open has its own queue, and a line already
waiting in it is not queued again.
.PP
The program keeps the tether.
appfs's processes are in a group and file descriptor table of their
own; when the program goes, or drops the tether, the posted file is
taken away and they go too.
.SH EXAMPLES
wm/man serves
.BR ctl ,
.B state
and
.BR view ;
.IR tools9p (8)
mounts
.B #sman/fs
at
.B /mnt/man
for the man tool.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/appfs.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR post9p (2),
.IR styxservers (2)
